Chapter 6B research completed for Andrés Manuel López Obrador, Mexico, 2022. No score assigned and no other chapter researched.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Informe de la pobreza multidimensional en México, 2022","publisher":"CONEVAL","publication_date":"2023-08","url":"https://www.coneval.org.mx/InformesPublicaciones/Documents/Pobreza_Multidimensional_2022.pdf","claim":"Multidimensional poverty fell from 41.9% in 2018 to 36.3% in 2022, but extreme poverty rose in absolute terms from 8.7 to 9.1 million people.","locator":"pp. 23–24, lines 604–616","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-001","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.coneval.org.mx/InformesPublicaciones/Documents/Pobreza_Multidimensional_2022.pdf|pp.23-24|poverty-2018-2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.1","6B.2","6B.10"],"source_type":"official_statistical_evaluation","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Mexico’s statutory poverty evaluator using ENIGH data and documented methodology.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly measures 2022 and compares it with the 2018 baseline.","ruler_attribution":"Federal social-policy direction and transfers were under López Obrador, but outcomes also reflect labor-market recovery, state governments, and post-pandemic conditions.","contrary_evidence":["Extreme poverty increased in absolute numbers.","Aggregate poverty reduction does not establish equal service access or causal attribution."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.2","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Informe de la pobreza multidimensional en México, 2022","publisher":"CONEVAL","publication_date":"2023-08","url":"https://www.coneval.org.mx/InformesPublicaciones/Documents/Pobreza_Multidimensional_2022.pdf","claim":"Government transfers helped reduce poverty exposure, but CONEVAL found transfers insufficient to reverse deep social-rights deficits among the poorest decile.","locator":"p. 24, lines 624–632; p. 154, lines 6160–6161","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-002","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.coneval.org.mx/InformesPublicaciones/Documents/Pobreza_Multidimensional_2022.pdf|p24|transfers-insufficient","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.10"],"source_type":"official_statistical_evaluation","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary national evaluation with explicit analysis of transfer effects and limitations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Uses 2022 household data and the 2018–2022 policy period.","ruler_attribution":"Transfers were a central federal administration instrument; structural service deficits were shared across federal and subnational systems.","contrary_evidence":["CONEVAL’s counterfactual shows poverty would have been higher without transfers.","Transfers did not resolve health, education, housing, and other rights gaps."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Informe de la pobreza multidimensional en México, 2022","publisher":"CONEVAL","publication_date":"2023-08","url":"https://www.coneval.org.mx/InformesPublicaciones/Documents/Pobreza_Multidimensional_2022.pdf","claim":"Health-service deprivation worsened sharply: national health-access deprivation reached 39.1% in 2022, with rural deprivation at 54.8%; Chiapas, Oaxaca, and Guerrero were especially affected.","locator":"pp. 159–162, lines 6363–6395 and 6504–6508","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-003","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.coneval.org.mx/InformesPublicaciones/Documents/Pobreza_Multidimensional_2022.pdf|pp159-162|health-deprivation","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.10"],"source_type":"official_statistical_evaluation","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct ENIGH-based measurement with rural, urban, and state-level disaggregation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 outcome evidence with 2018 and 2020 comparisons.","ruler_attribution":"Health-system redesign and federal coverage policy were substantially executive responsibilities, though implementation depended on states and inherited fragmentation.","contrary_evidence":["The administration was undertaking IMSS-Bienestar reform and pandemic recovery.","The evidence does not isolate federal policy from COVID-19 disruption."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Informe de la pobreza multidimensional en México, 2022","publisher":"CONEVAL","publication_date":"2023-08","url":"https://www.coneval.org.mx/InformesPublicaciones/Documents/Pobreza_Multidimensional_2022.pdf","claim":"Indigenous deprivation remained severe: among Indigenous-language speakers, the share with at least three social deprivations rose from 60.3% in 2018 to 68.2% in 2022.","locator":"pp. 88–89, lines 3442–3460","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-004","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.coneval.org.mx/InformesPublicaciones/Documents/Pobreza_Multidimensional_2022.pdf|pp88-89|indigenous-deprivation","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"],"source_type":"official_statistical_evaluation","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official disaggregated poverty estimates using two Indigenous-identification criteria.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct comparison of 2018 and 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Federal social programs and Indigenous policy were within presidential policy authority, while delivery was shared with states and communities.","contrary_evidence":["CONEVAL reports some aggregate poverty improvement among Indigenous groups.","The indicator measures deprivation, not the full range of dignity or opportunity."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Pobreza infantil y adolescente en México-2022","publisher":"UNICEF México and CONEVAL","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.unicef.org/mexico/informes/pobreza-infantil-y-adolescente-en-m%C3%A9xico-2022","claim":"Child and adolescent poverty declined from 50.3% in 2018 to 45.8% in 2022, but children remained the population group with the highest poverty levels and Indigenous children were especially disadvantaged.","locator":"Executive highlights and report description","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-005","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.unicef.org/mexico/informes/pobreza-infantil-y-adolescente-en-m%C3%A9xico-2022|highlights|child-poverty","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"],"source_type":"international_child_welfare_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Joint UNICEF–CONEVAL analysis focused specifically on children, adolescents, and social-protection effects.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly analyzes 2022 child poverty and the 2018–2022 change.","ruler_attribution":"Federal transfers and child policy were administratively attributable to the government, with substantial subnational delivery constraints.","contrary_evidence":["Child poverty remained much higher than the national average.","The report identifies continuing social-security and Indigenous disparities."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Teaching at the Right Level supports learning recovery in Mexico","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2022-03","url":"https://www.unicef.org/documents/teaching-right-level-supports-learning-recovery-mexico","claim":"A government-led Learning Camps initiative, supported by UNICEF and partners, targeted foundational literacy and numeracy gaps and reported measurable gains after severe COVID-era disruption.","locator":"Case-study description, lines 194–201","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-006","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.unicef.org/documents/teaching-right-level-supports-learning-recovery-mexico|lines194-201|learning-camps","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.2","6B.5","6B.6","6B.9"],"source_type":"international_program_case_study","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF documents the initiative and approach, but the page gives limited independent outcome detail.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Initiative and case study are situated in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The Ministry of Education led the program; UNICEF and other partners contributed technical support.","contrary_evidence":["The case study acknowledges deep pre-existing inequities and severe learning gaps.","Coverage and durability beyond the intervention are not established."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.5","6B.6","6B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Evaluación Estratégica de Salud. Primer Informe","publisher":"CONEVAL","publication_date":"2022-09","url":"https://www.coneval.org.mx/InformesPublicaciones/Paginas/Mosaicos/Evaluacion_Estrategica_de_Salud_informe_2022.aspx","claim":"CONEVAL’s 2022 strategic health evaluation diagnosed pandemic impacts, health-system challenges, and design/coherence problems in programs serving people without social security.","locator":"Report description, lines 123–130","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-007","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.coneval.org.mx/InformesPublicaciones/Paginas/Mosaicos/Evaluacion_Estrategica_de_Salud_informe_2022.aspx|lines123-130|health-system-evaluation","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.4","6B.5","6B.6","6B.9"],"source_type":"official_program_evaluation","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent statutory evaluator explicitly assessing system capacity and program coherence.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published in September 2022 and assesses current system conditions.","ruler_attribution":"The federal executive controlled major health-system reforms and program design, but service delivery was institutionally fragmented.","contrary_evidence":["The evaluation itself represents an institutional measurement and correction capacity.","It does not by itself prove the president ignored the findings."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.5","6B.6","6B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"El cuento de la igualdad en México","publisher":"IMCO","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://imco.org.mx/el-cuento-de-la-igualdad-en-mexico/","claim":"Compared with 2022, 2023 budgets provided only small real increases or reductions for women’s violence-prevention programs, shelters, and victim services; the executive also eliminated Full-Time Schools, affecting childcare and working mothers.","locator":"Analysis paragraphs 64–67","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-008","canonical_fact_key":"https://imco.org.mx/el-cuento-de-la-igualdad-en-mexico/|paragraphs64-67|women-programs-schools","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.3","6B.4","6B.8"],"source_type":"civil_society_budget_analysis","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Named-budget analysis by a recognized Mexican policy institute; comparison is partly forward-looking from 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Uses the 2022 budget as the comparison baseline; the school-program decision occurred during the period.","ruler_attribution":"Federal budget and executive program decisions were directly attributable, though Congress approved appropriations.","contrary_evidence":["The analysis notes replacement funding through La Escuela es Nuestra.","Budget allocation alone does not establish service outcomes."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.4","6B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"La evaluación en México, una política fragmentada","publisher":"Blanca Odille López Rodríguez, UNAM","publication_date":"2022-10-21","url":"https://www.revistas.unam.mx/index.php/rep/article/view/83742","claim":"A 2022 academic review characterized Mexico’s monitoring and evaluation architecture as fragmented, administratively inefficient, and marked by duplication, while proposing stronger coordination and institutionalization.","locator":"Article abstract, lines 41–64","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-009","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.revistas.unam.mx/index.php/rep/article/view/83742|lines41-64|fragmented-evaluation","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.4","6B.5","6B.9"],"source_type":"peer_reviewed_academic_analysis","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"University-published scholarly analysis with explicit methodology and bibliographic record.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published during the target year and evaluates the national institutional architecture.","ruler_attribution":"The architecture spans multiple administrations and branches; attribution to López Obrador is partial rather than exclusive.","contrary_evidence":["The existence of CONEVAL and other monitoring bodies shows institutional capacity rather than total absence of evaluation."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.5","6B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico's poorest receiving less government funds under president who brought poor to the fore","publisher":"Associated Press","publication_date":"2024-05-07","url":"https://apnews.com/article/mexico-poverty-election-lopez-obrador-182061c29209528ea5553ce89d6db09c","claim":"Reporting on the administration’s social-program architecture found that redirecting Prospera resources toward a universal older-person pension benefited broader income groups and could reach fewer of the poorest children; critics also raised clientelism and stigma concerns.","locator":"Paragraphs 2030–2071","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-010","canonical_fact_key":"https://apnews.com/article/mexico-poverty-election-lopez-obrador-182061c29209528ea5553ce89d6db09c|paragraphs2030-2071|targeting-shift","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.3","6B.7","6B.8"],"source_type":"investigative_press_report","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"AP reporting includes named researchers and field observations but is published after the target year and is not a controlled evaluation.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Reports a policy structure operating during 2022, with publication in 2024.","ruler_attribution":"The shift from Prospera to universal pensions was a central administration policy choice.","contrary_evidence":["Universal pensions expanded coverage and were politically popular across the electorate.","The report does not establish that benefits were explicitly conditioned on political loyalty."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.7","6B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexican president inaugurates centralized 'super pharmacy' to supply medicines to all of Mexico","publisher":"Associated Press","publication_date":"2023-12-29","url":"https://apnews.com/article/mexico-pharmaceutical-distribution-shortages-drugs-bc4e8f0a000b79f712c74b7fa92cf8e4","claim":"Medicine shortages persisted in 2022, with approximately 12.5 million prescriptions unfilled; AP reported that centralized purchasing decisions under López Obrador created distribution bottlenecks, while inherited infrastructure problems also contributed.","locator":"Paragraphs 2272–2291","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-011","canonical_fact_key":"https://apnews.com/article/mexico-pharmaceutical-distribution-shortages-drugs-bc4e8f0a000b79f712c74b7fa92cf8e4|paragraphs2272-2291|medicine-shortages","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.6"],"source_type":"reported_health_investigation","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"AP cites health officials, WHO’s Mexico representative, academics, and the Zero Shortages group; some figures are reported retrospectively.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Explicitly reports the 2022 prescription-shortage figure.","ruler_attribution":"Centralized procurement and distribution were executive policy choices, while longstanding corruption and infrastructure deficits were inherited constraints.","contrary_evidence":["The administration secured COVID-19 vaccines effectively by late 2021.","WHO’s representative said centralized warehousing could work with proper logistics."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.6"]}

Research summary:

- Documents discovered: approximately 47 plausible candidates.
- Documents opened: 24 underlying pages, reports, or PDFs.
- Accepted: 11 new source-claim units.
- Rejected or context-only: 12 candidates/records.
- Access-blocked or fetch-failed: 5, including one government page returning HTTP 403.
- Reused evidence: 2 authority records from the supplied index; they were not counted as newly discovered Chapter 6B documents.
- Distinct organizations: CONEVAL, UNICEF, INEGI, IMCO, UNAM, Associated Press, El País, Frontiers, and the Mexican executive branch.
- Source types: official statistics/evaluations, international-organization reports, academic analysis, civil-society budget analysis, and press investigations.

Lens coverage:

- 6B.1: covered through stated welfare priorities, transfers, and poverty outcomes; direct evidence on sincerity versus political messaging remains limited.
- 6B.2: covered strongly across poverty, health, education, medicine access, and social protection.
- 6B.3: covered through children, Indigenous groups, rural populations, and women.
- 6B.4: covered through health-system design, medicine procurement, budget decisions, and administrative capacity.
- 6B.5: covered through CONEVAL monitoring, ENIGH measurement, UNICEF learning evaluation, and UNAM’s evaluation-architecture critique.
- 6B.6: covered through COVID recovery, learning loss, and medicine shortages.
- 6B.7: partially covered. Evidence raises targeting and clientelism concerns, but no decisive 2022 proof of benefits being conditioned on political loyalty was found.
- 6B.8: covered through Indigenous, child, gender, rural, and dignity-related evidence.
- 6B.9: partially covered. Evaluation institutions and Learning Camps provide positive institutional evidence, but durability and post-administration survival remain uncertain.
- 6B.10: covered with mixed evidence: lower aggregate poverty and child poverty, offset by worsening health access, persistent extreme poverty, and entrenched group disparities.

The cumulative manifest could not be written because the filesystem is read-only and the patch attempt was rejected. The cumulative manifest fallback is the supplied prior entries `AMLO22-R01`, `AMLO22-R02`, `AMLO22-R04`–`AMLO22-R08`, `AMLO22-R12`, plus new entries `WEB-6B-001` through `WEB-6B-011` above.

A further Chapter 6B-specific wave would have limited marginal value unless targeted narrowly at the residual gaps: verified 2022 evidence of welfare conditionality/clientelism, audited implementation quality for individual programs, and longitudinal evidence on whether reforms became durable institutions. Further broad searching would mostly duplicate the already well-covered mixed outcome record.
